fans eagerly awaiting the december 17th  release of netflix's the witcher season  2 can now get their monsters mages and  magic fix in the form of director quang  ilhan's animated spin-off film the  witcher nightmare of the wolf here's  everything you need to know about the  prequel's poignant ending  the prequel focuses on the backstory of  vessemir geralt of rivia's mentor and  father figure who's set to be played by  kim bodnia in the live-action series  second season this prequel chronicles  the events that led him to become a  witcher as well as the experiences that  forced him to rethink his beliefs  priorities and values  since those values were passed on to  geralt the film serves to bridge the gap  between the geralt of season 1 and the  geralt created over the course of the  series source material by andrei  sapkovsky although the witcher nightmare  of the wolf is not strictly speaking a  depiction of anything from the novels  the film's writer beau dimeo was intent  on creating something that worked within  the canon and franchise  in an interview with cbr dimeo discussed  the challenge of delving into storylines  the books doesn't necessarily  investigate while simultaneously staying  true to their character's narrative he  explained i started thinking about who  was vesimir at the start of his career  before the horrible things that happened  with the witchers in the canon in  exploring these untapped arcs and  focusing on what he called that  unanswerable mystery in the witcher  nightmare of the wolf touches on a  number of themes integral to sapkovsky's  original works and helped give fans a  deeper understanding of the dynamics of  both the continent and the various  factions within it it's difficult not to  read sapkowski's original saga as a  commentary on the tumultuous and  war-torn history of his home country of  poland nightmare of the wolf foregrounds  themes such as class and racial divide  societal constructs and the symbolic  relationship between fear and violence  ignorance and hate  when we first meet vesemir he's a young  viral witcher equivalent of a bro dude  focused mainly on collecting coin and  living it up when he can but the hunky  hexer's laissez-faire attitude belies a  deep-seated desire to rise above the  impoverished social status of his youth  flashbacks to vesimir's childhood reveal  that he was born into servitude and  longed for nothing more than to free  himself and his childhood love ilyana  from the shackles of their station at  the beginning of his narrative we're  shown a vesimir who still harbors hatred  for the nobility he's so envious but by  the end of nightmare of the wolf he's  determined to live for something more  meaningful than glory coin and status  aliana faces her own fair share of  classism when she grows into adulthood  even as she rises through the ranks of  society in vesimir's absence eliana  marries the son of the noble family that  claims her and upon his death inherits  his title advisor to the king it soon  becomes clear however that even as lady  zerps she has difficulty commanding the  respect her position should by right  demand though her late husband clearly  saw her as an equal the nobility and  their ilk never do in the end it's  eliana's untimely death that finally  forces vesimir to confront the  misguidedness of his priorities  vesimir's priorities of coin and glory  are shared and reinforced by his own  mentor deglin when vestemir tells the  frightened abandoned orphans at  caramoran that they'll gain strength  purpose and respect as the next  generation of witchers he's merely  parroting what he was told by deaglin as  a child the need for this kind of  purpose in life is exactly what drives  deglin to engage in the mutagenic  alchemy for which the witchers are  ultimately condemned and nearly wiped  out he tells vesimir monsters were going  extinct i had to protect our way of life  deaglin feels forced to create new  monsters because it's only mankind's  reliance on the witchers for protection  from the world's scarier beasts that  keeps them from turning on and hunting  down witchers now they finally have  turned on the witchers with a little  push from the witcher hating mage tetra  gilchrist though ulyana doesn't agree  with deglan's approach she does tell  vesimir that war was inevitable as she  puts it killing is easier than  tolerating on the surface tetra is the  most outspoken racist in nightmare of  the wolf tetra even goes so far as to  publish propaganda pamphlets denouncing  witchers whom she views as corrupt  mutations in contrast to her pure mage  lineage as rogues without virtue it's  not easy being a witcher  trust me  i'd know in the end we learned there's  more to tetra's hateful conviction than  meets the eye her fervent hatred of  witchers stems from her past experience  with a single witcher an extremist who  tricked a priest into believing that  tetra's mother was possessing him in the  same way that vesamir's experience with  one particular nobleman as a child  colored his understanding of the world  from there on out tetra's traumatic  experience with a radical rogue witcher  in turn radicalized her might have  slightly underestimated you  slightly  the witcher nightmare of the wolf  highlights the interconnected cyclical  nature of many of society's worst  impulses ignorance begets fear fear  begets hate hate begets violence and so  on in the end only ilyana seems capable  of breaking the cycle when she asks  vesimir to forgive the tragically  mutated kitsu for her murder nightmare  of the wolf repeatedly explores the idea  of illusion both magical and  metaphorical for most of his life  vesimir is motivated by the illusion of  wealth and status though as a witcher he  obtains both it's a superficial victory  and one that doesn't ultimately bring  him happiness moreover in the end it's a  literal illusion that causes him to kill  the only woman he ever loved although  it's vesemir who falls victim to the  most devastating illusion of all it's  tetra who delivers the film's message to  viewers she tells vesemir of the mages  practice of casting illusions around  undesirable foods making them taste  better before going on to explain that  the telltale sign of an illusion is its  bitter aftertaste  there's more than a little irony  embedded in the reaction that some fans  had to the witcher season one creator's  decision not to use race or even gender  as a basis for casting canonical  characters when show runner lauren  hissrick was criticized for altering the  arguably only ever implied races of  various characters she had this to say  on twitter the witcher is really  interesting when it comes to depicting  racism because it's about species not  skin color what makes characters other  is the shape of their ears height etc in  the books no one pays attention to skin  color in the series no one does either  period it's an instance of life  imitating art supposed die-hard fans of  sapovsky's work directly embodied and  made incarnate one of the saga's main  allegories if the witcher season 1  introduced audiences to the dynamics of  the story's key players then the witcher  nightmare of the wolf introduced them to  a deeper understanding of the dynamics  of the continent and its history and in  doing so open the door for the witcher  season 2 to answer some of the  philosophical questions it poses  i need to understand some things  although we don't discover which of the  camera and orphans is the young white  wolf until the last scene the impact the  events of the story have on his future  mentor give audiences a more thorough  understanding of the upbringing that  created the hero at the heart of the  netflix series but they hate us  they'll always be another monster  geralt  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  the witcher universe are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  you
 

